# Install on UBUNTU TOUCH as /etc/udev/rules.d/99-clover-no-sdcard-automount.rules
# (rootfs is read-only: sudo mount -o remount,rw / ... then remount,ro)
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#
# The Nura SD card's root filesystem uses metadata_csum, metadata_csum_seed,
# 64bit and orphan_present. Ubuntu Touch runs a 4.4 kernel which cannot
# maintain those, yet udisks2 auto-mounts the card on every boot and ext4
# replays the journal, writing metadata it cannot checksum correctly. After any
# unclean Nura shutdown this silently corrupts the card:
#
#     Group 18 block bitmap does not match checksum.
#     Orphan file (inode 12) block 65 is not clean.
#     pmOS_root: ********** WARNING: Filesystem still has errors **********
#
# e2fsck once saw 12 files where there were 33,151, and Nura booted to a black
# screen. This happened repeatedly before the cause was understood.
#
# UDISKS_IGNORE ALONE DOES NOT WORK. That was the original fix here and it was
# wrong. UDISKS_IGNORE only hides the device from the UI; it does not stop
# auto-mounting. Verified on 22 October 2026: the property was set --
#
#     udevadm info --query=property --name=/dev/mmcblk1p2
#       UDISKS_IGNORE=1
#
# -- and the card was still mounted READ-WRITE:
#
#     /dev/mmcblk1p2 on /media/phablet/pmOS_root type ext4 (rw,...,uhelper=udisks2)
#
# UDISKS_AUTO=0 is the property that actually disables auto-mounting. Both are
# set below: AUTO to stop the mount, IGNORE to also keep it out of the UI.
SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="mmcblk1*", ENV{UDISKS_AUTO}="0", ENV{UDISKS_IGNORE}="1"

# Verify after installing, with the card inserted:
#     udevadm info --query=property --name=/dev/mmcblk1p2 | grep UDISKS
#     mount | grep mmcblk1        # must print nothing
#
# When you DO need to read the card from Ubuntu Touch, always use:
#     sudo mount -o ro,noload /dev/mmcblk1p2 /mnt/nura
# Plain "-o ro" is NOT enough - noload is what suppresses the journal replay.
